explanations.E0062:
# E0062: optional used as if it were present

A field read, a method call, an index or a `for` needs a value, not an optional of one. An optional may be `None`, which has no fields, methods or elements.

## Why

Sisu has no null pointer errors at runtime: the checker makes the program say what happens when the optional is `None`. `?.` reads the member only when the optional holds a value, and gives `None` otherwise. In an assignment or a `push`, `?.` makes the change only when the optional holds a value, and does nothing otherwise. `if let` runs a block with the value only when it is present. `let ... else` and `?? return` unwrap the value and leave when it is `None`.

The checker first looks for the member on the value inside the optional, as `?.` does. When that value has no such member, as in `n.size()` on a `Node?` where `Node` has no `size`, or the member does not apply to it, the error is the one that `?.` gives, such as [E0063](E0063.md) or [E0058](E0058.md), not E0062: a `?.` would not fix it.

An assignment to an element, such as `m?.items[0] = 2`, writes into the array that `m?.items` holds. An `if let` of that array binds a copy, and you cannot write to a copy. The help names the object of the `?.` instead: `if let m = m { m.items[0] = 2 }` writes into the array of `m`. A write such as `c?.s.k = 2` gets the same help, where `c` is an optional class and `s` is a struct field. An `if let` of `c?.s` binds a copy, and `if let c = c { c.s.k = 2 }` writes into `c`.

A `push` onto an optional array, such as `xs.push(2)` on an `[i64]?`, gets no `if let` help, because an unwrapped array is a copy. The `?.` fix is the form that changes the array: `xs?.push(2)`. The help names the object of a `?.` only when that object stores the array, as in `bag?.items.push(4)`.

A struct is a value, so `?.` cannot write a field of a struct yet: `o?.n = 1` on an `In?`, where `In` is a struct, is an error. An `if let` would bind a copy, and the write would change only the copy. Replace the whole value: the help gives `o = Some(...)`, with the new struct in the place of `...`. The help shows only when the optional before `?.` is a place on one line that the write can replace.

An optional of an optional, such as a `Node??`, has two layers, and `?.` takes only one off: `n?.value` on a `Node??` reads `value` from a `Node?`, which may be `None` too. Peel the other layer first with `??`: `(n ?? None)?.value`. A `.` on a `Node??` gets the same `?? None` help, with no fix, when it reads a field or calls a method. It gets the help too for a field assignment, a compound assignment and a `push` onto a field. An index, a slice, an element write and a `for` get the `if let` help in its place. So does an argument written with `mut`, or given to a `mut` parameter. The peeled form fails there. On an `[i64]??`, a `push` gets no help and no fix: an unwrap gives a copy, and the peeled `push` gives E0021. The help adds one `?? None` for each extra layer: on a `Node???`, it gives `((n ?? None) ?? None)?.value`.

## Broken

```sisu
class Node {
    value: i64
    next: Node?
}

fn second(n: Node) -> i64 {
    n.next.value
}

fn main() {
    print(second(Node(value: 1, next: Node(value: 2, next: None))))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Node {
    value: i64
    next: Node?
}

fn second(n: Node) -> i64 {
    if let next = n.next {
        next.value
    } else {
        0
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(second(Node(value: 1, next: Node(value: 2, next: None))))
}
```

## Related

- [E0049](E0049.md): `?.` on a value that is not optional.

explanations.E0120:
# E0120: variant or method used as the other

The methods of a struct or an enum are called on a value, `e.eval()`. The variants of an enum are built through the enum, `Expr.Num(1)`. A method named through its type, or a variant called on a value, is an error.

## Why

Sisu has one way to build a variant and one way to call a method. So `Expr.eval` never reads as a function value, and `e.Num(1)` never reads as a method. The error names the form that works.

A call such as `Expr.eval(e)` has a fix when its first argument has the type. The fix moves that argument before the `.`: `e.eval()`. It is safe when the method takes the other arguments. It is unsafe when one of them does not fit, or stands at a `mut` parameter and has no `mut`. It is also unsafe when the method has type parameters of its own, as in `Tree.gm(t, 1)` for `fn gm<U>(self, u: U)`.

Any other form has no fix, since the value to call the method on is yours to choose. That is, the first argument has another type, is optional or has `mut`, or the call has a label, a type argument or no argument.

## Broken

```sisu
enum Expr {
    Num(i64)
}

fn make(e: Expr) -> Expr {
    e.Num(1)
}

fn main() {
    match make(Expr.Num(0)) {
        Num(n) => print(n)
    }
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
enum Expr {
    Num(i64)
}

fn make(e: Expr) -> Expr {
    match e {
        Num(n) => Expr.Num(n + 1)
    }
}

fn main() {
    match make(Expr.Num(0)) {
        Num(n) => print(n)
    }
}
```
